.\" Man page for ax25netd_agwpe.conf
.\"
.TH AX25NETD_AGWPE.CONF 5 "29 Sep 2026" "ax25-apps" "Linux System Administration"
.SH NAME
ax25netd_agwpe.conf \- configuration for ax25netd
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ax25netd_agwpe.conf
lists the AGWPE servers ("upstreams") which
.BR ax25netd (8)
connects to and exposes to its local clients.
.PP
This file is read by
.BR ax25netd (8),
and by a program served by libax25's userspace shim, but only to answer one
question: which of the ports in
.BR axports (5)
name an upstream of this machine.  The shim needs that at
.BR bind (2)
to tell an AGWPE port from a port of a local radio, and it asks the
.I name
fields below first, so that the answer costs a file read instead of a
connection.  Where the file does not have the name the shim asks the server
itself, which is the case whenever the server is on another host; it then
reaches it over the daemon's loop port (see
.BR ax25common.conf (5)),
because the
.BR AXSOCK_HOST " / " AXSOCK_PORT
environment variables, the shim's only choice of server, name the daemon
there by default.  Point them at one of these servers instead, and the
daemon is skipped.
.PP
Each line defines one upstream:
.PP
.RS
.I name host tcp-port
.RE
.PP
The
.I name
is shown in the reply to the AGWPE "port information" query.  The
.I host
and
.I tcp-port
are where the AGWPE server listens (Direwolf defaults to port 8000).
.PP
The
.I host
accepts IPv4 and IPv6 addresses and host names; IPv6 literals may be
written in the bracketed notation
.B [2001:db8::1]
(brackets are stripped before lookup).  A host beginning with
.B /
names a unix domain socket instead of a TCP port, useful to reach an
AGWPE server on the same machine without TCP; in that case the
.I tcp-port
column is ignored and may be written as
.B \-
or left out.  See
.B UNIX_SOCKET_UPSTREAMS
below for what a program has to offer before one of these is worth
writing.
.PP
The AGWPE port numbers handed out to the local clients are flat and depend
only on the order of the upstreams in this file, never on the channel
layout of the upstreams: upstream \fIi\fR (counting from 0) owns the ports
\fIi*16\fR .. \fIi*16+15\fR and its channel \fIc\fR is port
\fIi*16+c\fR.  The port list is learned from the upstream's reply to the
"port information" query, so the numbering survives an upstream restart
with a changed number of channels.  A single-channel Direwolf keeps its
port number 0.
.PP
This numbering is what a client sees.  Which upstream, and which of its
channels, a client uses is not named in this file but in
.BR axports (5):
an entry whose name is the
.I name
of an upstream here addresses channel 0 of that upstream, and the same name
with a
.BI : N
suffix addresses channel N.  So an upstream with two channels needs the two
entries
.I name
and
.IR name :1
there, and a program is pointed at the second one by that name
.RI ( "call \-r name:1 ..." ).
.PP
Each of those entries carries a callsign of its own, because
.BR axports (5)
refuses two entries with the same callsign.  That matches how an AGWPE radio
server is put together: its ports are given one SSID each, and the callsign
of the entry is the address that the channel transmits, which is the radio's
own configuration and not this file's \-
.I MYCALL
per channel in
.BR direwolf.conf (5).
.PP
An upstream named here that
.BR axports (5)
does not have an entry for has no ports, and nothing else says so:
.BR ax25netd (8)
warns about it at startup and goes on, because the other upstreams are
still worth serving.  It does not warn the other way round.  A port in
.BR axports (5)
with no upstream here may be a port of the kernel stack or of a WAMPES
node, neither of which this daemon has anything to do with.
The reserved name
.B loop
enables the virtual local loopback upstream (AGWPE port 255).  It needs no
host or tcp-port; a line
.PP
.RS
.B loop
.RE
.PP
or, for readability,
.PP
.RS
.B loop
.I - -
.RE
.PP
is enough.  Local AX.25 services reachable on the loop port must have a
matching "loop" entry in
.BR axports (5).
.PP
An optional line
.PP
.RS
.B auth
.I no|extern|yes
.RE
.PP
selects how much authentication loop port clients need.  The default is
.BR extern :
clients connecting over loopback (127.0.0.1 and ::1) are trusted, while any
other client must log in with credentials from
.BR ax25netd_agwpe_shadow.conf (5)
before it may issue any command.
.B yes
asks every client to log in, loopback included;
.B no
trusts every client.
.B required
and
.B always
are synonyms for
.BR yes ,
.B loop
for
.BR extern .
.PP
The loop port endpoint itself (the unix domain socket, the TCP port and
the socket group) is not configured here but in the shared
.BR ax25common.conf (5),
which
.BR ax25netd (8)
and its local clients read alike.
.PP
.SH UNIX_SOCKET_UPSTREAMS
An upstream host beginning with
.B /
is a unix domain socket, and the column is worth filling in only if the
program on the other end actually serves AGWPE over one.  A
.I "/..."
path that nothing listens on fails the same way a closed TCP port
does, which is at least a clear error, so this costs nothing to try; a
path that does listen but speaks a different protocol does not, and the
AGWPE handshake then times out or is answered as a bad packet.
.PP
The known programs at the time of writing:
.TP
.B direwolf
No.
.BR direwolf (1)
speaks AGWPE over TCP, not over a unix domain socket, and there is no
option to change that.  Reach it with an address and a port - it listens
on
.B 8000
by default - exactly as in the
.B localhost 8000
line below.
.TP
.B agwpe_unix
and other wrappers
Only if the wrapper in question documents a socket.  A file in
.IR /var/run
does not by itself make a program speak AGWPE: the common occupant of
such a path is a program's own IPC socket, which is not AGWPE.
.PP
So a unix domain upstream is the exception, and a
.B 8000
line is the normal case.  The path above is a shape, not a path any
distributed program uses; substitute the one your program documents.
.PP
An optional line
.PP
.RS
.B autoroute
.I yes|no
.RE
.PP
controls automatic digipeater resolution for connects that name no
digipeaters ("connect port dest", the AGWPE "C" and "c" commands).
When enabled (the default, mirroring the kernel AX.25 stack) the daemon
asks the
.BR ax25rtd (8)
route cache for the destination on the target port and uses the learned
path.  A connect that names its digipeaters (the "v" command) is never
touched, and a connect to the loop port is never resolved.  An absent
or unresponsive route daemon is not an error: the plain connect is sent
instead.
.PP
Blank lines and lines beginning with
.B #
are ignored.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
	auth	extern
	autoroute	yes
	radio0	localhost	8000
	radio1	192.168.1.20	8000
	radio2	[2001:db8::1]	8000
	radio3	/var/run/ax25/sockets/agwpe_radio1.sock	-
	loop	-	-
.fi
.SH FILES
.I @@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/ax25netd_agwpe.conf
.SH SEE ALSO
